{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>Naphtali is a hind let loose.</b> For the land of Naphtali ripens its fruits quickly, like this hind that was trapped and escapes from the trap and is now let loose, for then it runs the most." "<b>Who gives words of beauty.</b> To his Creator, for having saved him from death. And now, behold, he calls it in the masculine language — perhaps he is also likening it to a ram (איל), and it (אילה) is in every language [feminine]. Another interpretation: 'Who gives words of beauty' is attached to Naphtali — that he gives words of beauty to his Creator for the portion He gave him, like this hind that gives thanks to its Creator for having escaped from the trap. Another interpretation: 'Naphtali' — the land of Naphtali is like a hind that has been flayed, whose skin does not hold its flesh. And the word 'שלוחה' means flaying, as we translate [in Aramaic] 'וישלח' — 'and he flayed' (והפשיט). Thus the land of Naphtali does not hold most of the fruits that are in it. And this interpretation follows the way of midrash that the Sages, of blessed memory, drashed: Why is the Land of Israel called צבי ( gazelle)? To tell you: just as this gazelle, its skin does not hold its flesh, so too the Land of Israel does not hold its fruits. Another interpretation: 'Naphtali is a hind let loose' — the land of Naphtali is a land of desire, for it ripens quickly. And the word אילה is like אילות, and the word שלוחה is like שלחיך פרדס רמונים. 'Who gives words of beauty' — the meaning is beautiful branches, and the word אמרי is like: two or three grapes on the top of the אמיר.'"], :book :genesis, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:07:34.082954637Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}